\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The potential $V(x) = (x^2 - 1)^2$ has two stable attractors at $x = \pm 1$, separated by an unstable fixed point at $x = 0$. The shaded regions are the two basins of attraction. Initial conditions in Basin A converge to the left attractor; those in Basin B converge to the right attractor. Which long-run state the system reaches depends on its initial condition and any fluctuations large enough to cross the separatrix.}}{7}{figure.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Anatomy and design space of multi-agent debate. \textbf {(a)} The generic round structure shared across debate variants. Braces mark the steps governed by the \emph {interaction} and \emph {agreement} dimensions of \citet {motger2026taxonomy}; the \emph {participant} dimension concerns the agents themselves and applies throughout. \textbf {(b)} Three communication topologies for four agents. In the star, all communication passes through the shaded hub. \textbf {(c)} A memory window of $W = 2$. At round $t$, each agent sees only the two preceding rounds. The specific protocol used in this thesis, which instantiates these steps, is shown in \autoref {fig:protocol}.}}{10}{figure.2}%
